    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, can you help me understand the difference between a \"confidentiality period\" and a \"survival clause\" in a standard contract? I'm trying to make sure I don't miss anything important.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The confidentiality period and survival clause, two essential components of a standard contract, are quite similar, but not exactly the same. The confidentiality period is a fixed duration, usually specified in months or years, during which the parties must keep confidential any information exchanged during the contract negotiations. It's like a time-limited \"gloves off\" period, where both parties must refrain from sharing sensitive information with others. Think of it as a temporary \"giant's club\" – once the period ends, the gloves are off, and the information can be shared freely.\n\nOn the other hand, a survival clause, also known as a \"governing law\" clause, is a provision that ensures the contract remains in effect even if one or both parties cease to exist. It's like a \"goblin-proof\" clause, designed to withstand the test of time and even the demise of the parties involved. This clause typically specifies the jurisdiction and laws that will govern the contract, even if the parties are no longer in business.\n\nTo illustrate the difference, consider a contract between a human and a company owned by a goblin. The human might agree to a confidentiality period, but the goblin company might include a survival clause to ensure the contract remains in",
      "source": "target",
